// From the journal, not yet reached here (spec). What the editor and the
// interfaces built on these features are for:
//
// A block of code collapses to a sentence of English saying what it does,
// and the other way: written at the level of description that is preferred,
// mathematical symbols or words, "there exists" or `#.some`. A selection is
// operated on by naming what to do to it, in the language or in English.
//
// Highlighting says what calls a thing, not only the thing: red, green and
// blue highlighted as red, green and blue.
//
// Ambiguity is resolved in the editor, and the resolution may be kept so it
// is never asked again. If an isomorphism is already there and somebody is
// writing it again, they are told.
//
// Tests collapse inline. Every function has a button that times it. A
// version-control selector sits at each function in each class, and versions
// may be shown intermingled — `%1..5 field`, `<&VER..&VER> field` — with a
// key to walk them.
//
// Movement: up and down remember the column they left and go back to it
// while nothing has moved sideways; end-of-line stays at the end of the line
// while moving up and down; control with up and down moves the page by a
// line. A goto in the editor brings its label back a space, and puts it back
// when a type is written there instead. Inside a double `((` the editor asks
// for `((` and a tab, because `<<` may mean a subexpression.
//
// Eye tracking chooses which of two places is the one being worked in: side
// by side, whichever is looked at; overlaid, the one on top.
//
// Searching inside a thing, and again inside the result, drops what was
// searched before and keeps the sequence as a program that can be undone —
// which is how an executable or a codebase nobody wrote is explored.
//
// Text that is encrypted is edited as plain text and normalised back to
// encrypted, with the password asked for when it is used. Custom symbols
// extend the text as a type of their own, and paste as something that says
// they are custom.
//
// An icon generates the same file in the other formats underneath it; a
// status has one too. A chat may hold only what changed since it was last
// seen. Any object may be rendered as a table, with preferences for how
// kinds of things are drawn per person. A condition may be put on a source
// file — that its classes are sorted, the way the language index is.
//
// A choice a persona makes carries the quest that explains why it is theirs
// to make, and says whether its results are being kept for later.
//
// An interface finds the controls nothing is bound to and offers them.
// Structures are bound to controls — this key does this, copy and paste are
// a binding — and are imported by dragging or pasting them in. One key shows
// something about everything at once: every orbit in the structure, drawn by
// rolling a loop out into a line by varying the direction it is walked. A
// slider moves the level of description over an existing reading of it. What
// is ignored — where a structure came from, the embedding it is drawn in —
// is dropped on purpose, which drives two structures towards the same point
// so they can be compared.
//
// Drawing: put the graphs at the top with their lines invisible, draw them
// on hover and dim what surrounds them; the minimap ignores the reference.
// Dragging goes from one point of the background to another. If a mouse must
// be used, it locks onto the structure nearest it. An interface that is not
// a hundred percent wide is not a box: it is whatever shape fits the width
// available at that height, and what does not fit wraps.
//
// Exploration: a thing that wanders — worlds, items, whatever there is — and
// reminds, suggests and explores periodically, answering where has not been
// looked, where could be looked, and where it was decided not to look but
// looking is still wanted. Replay of what happened, in detail. Something
// that works while nobody is: it says when it found something that matters
// for an open question, it offers an answer to a function that is still
// being written and says what would follow from it, and it starts on what
// can be undone while the decision is still pending.
//
// Something may be included conditionally on whoever looks having access to
// it — a video that is paid for shows only to those who paid — which is a
// way around ownership in a structure, and obnoxious if used for the wrong
// things.
//
// Proofs are a way of deciding what is worth pursuing: a search and a memory
// of it. What they say about a space is in how the conclusion was reached as
// much as in the conclusion, so the questions are how that is taken
// advantage of, and what makes a proof different from an ordinary program. A
// tactic expands into the proof it stands for and folds back while it is
// unchanged. Tests are the same thing in a narrower case, so both are one
// mechanism: a prover generates every boolean implementation there is.
//
// Complexity has two halves: what a thing costs to run in sequence, and what
// it costs to run in parallel — the second is what a language far from ones
// and zeros buys. They are forming the model and using it: induction, and
// deduction. Robustness follows both, which makes two kinds of robustness.
//
// Applications install into the browser, for the desktop and for the phone.
//
// A beginner is given a whole setup and takes only the highlights out of it,
// so the rest can be decoded afterwards.
//
// Colour is a filter across a frequency, so the difference between two of
// them is one too.
//
// Modelling in two and three dimensions, generated rather than drawn.
//
// Interfaces from elsewhere plug into one modular system, which is what
// makes an interface reprogrammable at all.
